§§ `Rule 26 -- permission to appeal`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The keel's `o 1 r 2(5)(a)` says paragraphs (1) to (4) do not apply to an application for permission mentioned in `o 1 r 2(4)(c)`
-- (a revoked-Rules decision), and `o 1 r 2(5)(b)` reads "the time provided under this Rule" in paragraph (6)(a) as that rule's time.
-- Neither is threaded here (PLAN.md section 5, S-L2); see NOTES.md, Seams.

`o 19 r 26(1) the period for the application for permission` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 26(3)(b) the period for the application for permission after further arguments` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 26(6) the period for the application to the relevant appellate Court` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 26(8) the period for the opposing party's submissions` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 26(17) the period for the notice of appeal after permission is granted` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

-- Paragraph (1).  "Subject to paragraphs (2), (3) and (4) and any written law": where a request for further arguments has been made,
-- paragraph (3) fixes the time instead; a written law is outside these sources.
-- src:2513 | 26. —(1) Where permission to appeal against a decision is required, subject to paragraphs (2), (3) and (4) and any written law, a party must apply for such permission from the appellate Court and file and serve the application and the documents mentioned in paragraph (7) on all parties who have an interest in the appeal within 14 days after the date of the lower Court’s decision. (2) Where permission to appeal against a decision is required, the Judge who made the decision may hear further arguments in respect of the decision if any party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests for further arguments pursuant to section 29B of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act before the earliest of the following: ( a )
-- src:2514 | the time at which the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted;
-- src:2515 | ( b )
-- src:2516 | the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made;
-- src:2517 | ( c )
-- src:2518 | the time at which an application for permission to appeal against the decision is filed.
-- src:2519 | (3) If a request for further arguments in respect of a decision has been made under paragraph (2) — ( a )
-- src:2520 | an application for permission to appeal against the decision may not be filed against the decision until the Judge — (i)
-- src:2521 | affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing further arguments; or
-- src:2522 | (ii)
-- src:2523 | certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required; and
-- src:2524 | ( b )
-- src:2525 | the party seeking to apply for permission to appeal must file and serve the application for permission to appeal against the decision, and the documents mentioned in paragraph (7), within 14 days after the date the Judge — (i)
-- src:2526 | affirms, varies or sets aside the decision after hearing further arguments; or
-- src:2527 | (ii)
-- src:2528 | certifies, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required.
-- src:2529 | (4) To avoid doubt, paragraphs (2) and (3) do not affect section 29B of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act.
-- src:2530 | (5) Where permission to appeal under section 47(1) of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act is required, the Court of Appeal may grant permission to appeal against a decision of the Appellate Division, where — ( a )
-- src:2531 | the appeal will raise a point of law of public importance; and
-- src:2532 | ( b )
-- src:2533 | it is appropriate for the Court of Appeal to hear a further appeal, having regard to all relevant matters, including either or both of the following: (i)
-- src:2534 | whether a decision of the Court of Appeal is required to resolve the point of law;
-- src:2535 | (ii)
-- src:2536 | whether the interests of the administration of justice, either generally or in the particular case, require the Court of Appeal’s consideration of the point of law.
-- src:2537 | (6) Despite paragraphs (1), (2) and (3), where — ( a )
-- src:2538 | a party has made an application to the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal for permission to appeal against a decision of the General Division within the time provided under this Rule; and
-- src:2539 | ( b )
-- src:2540 | the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal has decided that the court from which permission to appeal must be obtained, or which may grant permission to appeal, is the Court of Appeal or the Appellate Division respectively,
-- src:2541 | that party may make an application to the relevant appellate Court for permission to appeal against the General Division’s decision mentioned in sub‑paragraph ( a ) within 14 days after the date of the Registrar’s notification of the Appellate Division’s decision or the Court of Appeal’s decision mentioned in sub‑paragraph ( b ).
-- src:2542 | (7) A party applying for permission under this Rule — ( a )
-- src:2543 | must, at the time of filing the application, file written submissions and any accompanying bundle of authorities; and
-- src:2544 | ( b )
-- src:2545 | may, at the time of filing the application, file a bundle of documents that are relevant to the application and that were filed in the proceedings below, such as the whole or a part of the notes of evidence, pleadings and affidavits.
-- src:2546 | (8) A party who wishes to oppose an application for permission under this Rule — ( a )
-- src:2547 | must file and serve written submissions and any accompanying bundle of authorities; and
-- src:2548 | ( b )
-- src:2549 | may, together with the written submissions mentioned in sub‑paragraph ( a ), file and serve a bundle of documents that are relevant to the application and that were filed in the proceedings below, such as the whole or a part of the notes of evidence, pleadings and affidavits,
-- src:2550 | within 14 days after the application, written submissions, any accompanying bundle of authorities and bundle of documents mentioned in paragraph (7) are served on the party.
-- src:2551 | (9) The application, written submissions, any accompanying bundles of authorities and bundles of documents must be served on all parties who have an interest in the appeal.
-- src:2552 | (10) The written submissions are subject to a page limit of 15 pages, unless the appellate Court otherwise orders, and must be in the form specified in any practice directions issued by the Registrar.
-- src:2553 | (11) The bundle of documents is subject to a page limit of 25 pages.
-- src:2554 | (12) The appellate Court may allow the page limit mentioned in paragraph (10) to be exceeded — ( a )
-- src:2555 | in special circumstances; and
-- src:2556 | ( b )
-- src:2557 | unless the appellate Court otherwise orders under paragraph (13), upon the payment of the fees and additional fees prescribed for the filing of pages in excess of the page limit.
-- src:2558 | (13) The appellate Court may upon application waive, refund, defer or apportion the payment of the fees mentioned in paragraph (12)( b ).
-- src:2559 | (14) There must not be more than one set of submissions for each party or set of parties represented by the same firm of solicitors unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2560 | (15) No affidavit is to be filed in an application for permission to appeal without the permission of the appellate Court.
-- src:2561 | (16) No oral arguments are to be made in an application for permission to appeal unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2562 | (17) Where permission to appeal is granted, the applicant must file and serve on all parties who have an interest in the appeal the notice of appeal in Form 35 within 14 days after the date of the decision granting permission.
-- src:2563 | (18) The appellate Court may extend the time for filing and serving an application for permission to appeal.
@ref O 19 r 26 (src:2513-2563)
GIVEN `permission to appeal against the decision is required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the application is made to the appellate Court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a written law provides otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the written submissions and bundle of authorities are filed with the application as paragraph (7) requires` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the application and the documents mentioned in paragraph (7) are served on all parties who have an interest in the appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date the application is filed` IS A DATE
      `the date the application is served` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 4 the last day as extended or shortened` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 26(1) the application for permission is filed and served as required` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`permission to appeal against the decision is required`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where permission to appeal against a decision is required" -- a "where" that is not met
        IF NOT (`the application is made to the appellate Court`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a party must apply for such permission from the appellate Court" -- the application went to another court
        IF `a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)`
            THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "subject to paragraphs (2), (3) and (4)" -- paragraph (3)(b) fixes the time after further arguments
        IF `a written law provides otherwise`
            THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "and any written law"
        IF NOT (`the written submissions and bundle of authorities are filed with the application as paragraph (7) requires` AND `the application and the documents mentioned in paragraph (7) are served on all parties who have an interest in the appeal`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "file and serve the application and the documents mentioned in paragraph (7) on all parties who have an interest in the appeal"
        IF NOT (`o 19 r 8(3) the filing and the service are done by the last day` `the date the application is filed` `the date the application is served` `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` `o 3 r 4 the last day as extended or shortened`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "within 14 days after the date of the lower Court’s decision" -- outside a period the text fixes in its own words
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Paragraph (2).  The times are compared as a count of minutes since midnight at the start of 1 January 2000, so that a request
-- made at 3.00 p.m. on the same day as an extraction at 5.00 p.m. is "before" it.  "The 15th day after the date on which the decision
-- is made" is read as the decision date plus 15 days (O 3 r 3(3): the period begins immediately after that date), and "before" that
-- day as before its first minute (fork F5 in NOTES.md).
GIVEN m IS A Moment
GIVETH A NUMBER
`o 19 r 26(2) the minute count of a moment` MEANS
    ((m's `day of the moment` MINUS (Date 1 1 2000)) TIMES 1440) PLUS m's `minutes after midnight`

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
      b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`o 19 r 26(2) the smaller of two counts` MEANS
    IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

GIVEN `the date the decision is made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`o 19 r 26(2)(b) the start of the 15th day after the date the decision is made` MEANS
    (((`the date the decision is made` PLUS 15) MINUS (Date 1 1 2000)) TIMES 1440)

GIVEN `the moment the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted` IS A MAYBE Moment
      `the date the decision is made` IS A DATE
      `the moment an application for permission to appeal against the decision is filed` IS A MAYBE Moment
GIVETH A NUMBER
`o 19 r 26(2) the earliest of the three times` MEANS
    `o 19 r 26(2) the smaller of two counts`
        (CONSIDER `the moment the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted`
         WHEN JUST x THEN `o 19 r 26(2) the minute count of a moment` x
         WHEN NOTHING THEN `o 19 r 26(2)(b) the start of the 15th day after the date the decision is made` `the date the decision is made`)
        (`o 19 r 26(2) the smaller of two counts`
            (`o 19 r 26(2)(b) the start of the 15th day after the date the decision is made` `the date the decision is made`)
            (CONSIDER `the moment an application for permission to appeal against the decision is filed`
             WHEN JUST y THEN `o 19 r 26(2) the minute count of a moment` y
             WHEN NOTHING THEN `o 19 r 26(2)(b) the start of the 15th day after the date the decision is made` `the date the decision is made`))

@ref O 19 r 26(2) (src:2513-2518)
GIVEN `permission to appeal against the decision is required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests further arguments pursuant to section 29B of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the moment the request is made` IS A Moment
      `the moment the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted` IS A MAYBE Moment
      `the date the decision is made` IS A DATE
      `the moment an application for permission to appeal against the decision is filed` IS A MAYBE Moment
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 26(2) the Judge who made the decision may hear further arguments` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`permission to appeal against the decision is required`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where permission to appeal against a decision is required" -- a "where" that is not met
        IF NOT (`a party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests further arguments pursuant to section 29B of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if any party to the hearing, or the Judge, requests for further arguments pursuant to section 29B" -- an "if" that is not met
        IF `o 19 r 26(2) the minute count of a moment` `the moment the request is made` LESS THAN `o 19 r 26(2) the earliest of the three times` `the moment the judgment or order relating to the decision is extracted` `the date the decision is made` `the moment an application for permission to appeal against the decision is filed`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "before the earliest of the following" -- the request was not made before the earliest of the times in (a), (b) and (c)

-- Paragraph (3): once a request for further arguments is made, no application for permission may be filed until the Judge has disposed of it.
-- The "until" bar as its own function (ruling of 2026-11-05 (d)): JUST TRUE before the Judge has disposed of the request, JUST FALSE after.
@ref O 19 r 26(3)(a) (src:2519-2528)
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments, or has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 26(3)(a) the application for permission to appeal cannot yet be filed against the decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)`)
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If a request for further arguments in respect of a decision has been made under paragraph (2)" -- an "if" that is not met
        IF `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments, or has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required`
            THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may not be filed against the decision until the Judge" -- the Judge has done (i) or (ii)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- It never says that the application MAY be filed: JUST FALSE before the Judge disposes of the request, NOTHING after.
@ref O 19 r 26(3)(a) (src:2519-2523)
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments, or has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 26(3)(a) the application for permission to appeal may be filed against the decision` MEANS
    IF `o 19 r 26(3)(a) the application for permission to appeal cannot yet be filed against the decision` `a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)` `the Judge has affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments, or has certified or is deemed to have certified that no further arguments are required` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may not be filed against the decision until the Judge"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): paragraph (3)(a) says only when an application may NOT yet be filed; the time to file is paragraph (3)(b)

-- The date after which the 14 days of paragraph (3)(b) run: the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision, or the date the Judge certified (or is deemed to have certified).
@ref O 19 r 26(3)(b) (src:2525-2528)
GIVEN `the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the date the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`o 19 r 26(3)(b) the date after which the 14 days for the application run` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date the Judge affirmed, varied or set aside the decision after hearing further arguments`
    WHEN JUST affirmed THEN
        CONSIDER `the date the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required`
        WHEN JUST certified THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the two events are alternatives (i) and (ii) and the text does not say which counts when both are given
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST affirmed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the date the Judge certified, or is deemed to have certified, that no further arguments are required`

@ref O 19 r 26(3)(b) (src:2524-2528)
GIVEN `a request for further arguments has been made under paragraph (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
