§§ `Rule 30 -- documents to be filed`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

`o 19 r 30(3) the period for written grounds of decision` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 12
    `period unit` IS Weeks
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 30(3)(b) the period of notice to strike out` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 30(4) the period for the appellant's documents` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 8
    `period unit` IS Weeks
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 30(5) the period for the appellant's documents on an appeal from the Appellate Division` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 8
    `period unit` IS Weeks
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 30(8)(a) the period for the request for directions` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 30(9) the period for the respondent's documents` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 28
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 30(11) the period for the appellant's reply documents` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

-- src:2588 | 30. —(1) The lower Court must issue its written grounds of decision after the notice of appeal has been filed if the lower Court has not already done so. (2) The Registry must notify the parties when the record of proceedings is ready for collection.
-- src:2589 | (3) If no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks after the date of filing of the notice of appeal — ( a )
-- src:2590 | the appellant must apply in writing to the Registrar to proceed with the appeal and for a copy of the record of proceedings;
-- src:2591 | ( b )
-- src:2592 | if the appellant fails to do so, the respondent may apply in writing to the Registrar to proceed with the appeal and for a copy of the record of proceedings, or may give the appellant 14 days’ written notice of the respondent’s intention to strike out the appeal; and
-- src:2593 | ( c )
-- src:2594 | after the expiry of the 14 days’ notice period mentioned in sub‑paragraph ( b ), the respondent may apply to strike out the appeal. [S 37/2024 wef 01/02/2024]
-- src:2595 | (4) Except for a case to which paragraph (5) applies, the appellant must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2596 | the record of appeal;
-- src:2597 | ( b )
-- src:2598 | the appellant’s Case;
-- src:2599 | ( c )
-- src:2600 | the appellant’s core bundle of documents, with the written judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court (if any) and the extracted order of the lower Court in a separate volume; and
-- src:2601 | ( d )
-- src:2602 | the appellant’s bundle of authorities,
-- src:2603 | within 8 weeks after the date on which the Registry informs the parties that the record of proceedings is available.
-- src:2604 | (5) In the case of an appeal to the Court of Appeal against a decision of the Appellate Division, the record of appeal filed in the Appellate Division is treated as the record of appeal in the appeal to the Court of Appeal, and the appellant must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2605 | the appellant’s supplemental record of appeal comprising — (i)
-- src:2606 | the notice of appeal to the Court of Appeal;
-- src:2607 | (ii)
-- src:2608 | the certificate of payment for security for costs;
-- src:2609 | (iii)
-- src:2610 | the written judgment or grounds of decision of the Appellate Division or, if there is no judgment or grounds of decision, the certified minute sheet of the Appellate Division;
-- src:2611 | (iv)
-- src:2612 | the extracted order of the Appellate Division;
-- src:2613 | (v)
-- src:2614 | the order granting permission to appeal to the Court of Appeal;
-- src:2615 | (vi)
-- src:2616 | the parties’ Cases filed in the Appellate Division; and
-- src:2617 | (vii)
-- src:2618 | all documents filed in the Appellate Division (so far as they are relevant to the matter decided and the nature of the appeal);
-- src:2619 | ( b )
-- src:2620 | the appellant’s Case;
-- src:2621 | ( c )
-- src:2622 | the appellant’s core bundle of documents, with the written judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court and the extracted order of the lower Court in a separate volume; and
-- src:2623 | ( d )
-- src:2624 | the appellant’s bundle of authorities,
-- src:2625 | within 8 weeks after the date on which the Registry informs the parties that the record of proceedings is available.
-- src:2626 | (6) Where the appellant fails to file and serve the record of appeal (including, where required under paragraph (5)( a ), the supplemental record of appeal), the core bundle of documents or the appellant’s Case within the specified time, the appeal is deemed withdrawn unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2627 | (7) Where an appeal is deemed withdrawn pursuant to paragraph (6) and if all the parties to the appeal consent to the payment of the security for costs provided under Rule 27 to the appellant or the respondent, the appellant or the respondent (as the case may be) must file the document signifying such consent signed by the parties and in such event, the security for costs provided under Rule 27 must be paid to the appellant or the respondent (as the case may be) and any solicitor’s undertaking is discharged.
-- src:2628 | (8) Where an appeal is deemed withdrawn pursuant to paragraph (6) and if there are any outstanding issues as to costs or other matters that remain between the parties to the appeal — ( a )
-- src:2629 | the appellant or any party to the appeal may, within 14 days after the date that the appeal is deemed withdrawn, request in writing to the appellate Court for directions on those issues;
-- src:2630 | ( b )
-- src:2631 | no oral arguments are to be made in a request under sub‑paragraph ( a ) unless the appellate Court otherwise directs; and
-- src:2632 | ( c )
-- src:2633 | the Registrar of the Supreme Court may, upon receiving a request under sub‑paragraph ( a ), give directions on the making of written submissions for the request.
-- src:2634 | (9) The respondent must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2635 | the respondent’s Case;
-- src:2636 | ( b )
-- src:2637 | the respondent’s core bundle of documents (if necessary); and
-- src:2638 | ( c )
-- src:2639 | the respondent’s bundle of authorities,
-- src:2640 | within 28 days after the appellant serves the documents mentioned in paragraph (4) or (5) on the respondent.
-- src:2641 | (10) Where the respondent fails to file and serve the respondent’s Case within the specified time, the respondent is not allowed to make submissions at the hearing of the appeal unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2642 | (11) The appellant must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2643 | the appellant’s Reply (if any);
-- src:2644 | ( b )
-- src:2645 | the second core bundle (if necessary); and
-- src:2646 | ( c )
-- src:2647 | the appellant’s second bundle of authorities (if any),
-- src:2648 | within 14 days after the respondent’s Case is served on the appellant.
-- src:2649 | (12) Where there is more than one appellant in an appeal, all the appellants may join in one appellants’ Case and in one appellants’ Reply.
-- src:2650 | (13) Where there is more than one respondent in an appeal, all the respondents may join in one respondents’ Case.
-- src:2651 | (14) A party whose interest in the appeal is passive (such as a stakeholder, a trustee or an executor) is not required to file a separate Case but should ensure that that party’s position is explained in one of the Cases filed.
-- src:2652 | (15) The appellant and the respondent may seek directions from the appellate Court to file a joint Case where there are special circumstances.
-- src:2653 | (16) All parties to 2 or more related appeals to be heard together must try to agree on filing a single Case for each party and on the timelines for such filing.
-- src:2654 | (17) Where the parties are unable to agree as mentioned in paragraph (16), they must request in writing for a case conference before the appellate Court or seek directions from the appellate Court.
-- src:2655 | (18) Where the parties have agreed as mentioned in paragraph (16), they must inform the Registrar in writing of the timelines agreed, and seek the approval of the timelines by the appellate Court.
-- src:2656 | (19) Where there are 2 or more appeals arising from the same judgment, the parties must file a joint record of appeal.
-- src:2657 | (20) No documents other than what has been set out in this Rule may be filed unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2658 | (21) No written submissions or skeletal arguments may be filed before or at the appeal unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
@ref O 19 r 30 (src:2588-2658)
GIVEN `the notice of appeal has been filed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the lower Court has already issued its written grounds of decision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 30(1) the lower Court must issue its written grounds of decision` MEANS
    IF `the notice of appeal has been filed` AND NOT (`the lower Court has already issued its written grounds of decision`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "after the notice of appeal has been filed if the lower Court has not already done so" -- an "after" and an "if" that are not met

@ref O 19 r 30(2) (src:2588)
GIVEN `the record of proceedings is ready for collection` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 30(2) the Registry must notify the parties that the record of proceedings is ready for collection` MEANS
    IF `the record of proceedings is ready for collection`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Registry must notify the parties when the record of proceedings is ready for collection" -- a "when" that is not met

@ref O 19 r 30(3) (src:2589-2594)
GIVEN `the date the written grounds of decision are issued` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 30(3) no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date the written grounds of decision are issued`
    WHEN JUST issued THEN
        IF issued AT MOST `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks after the date of filing of the notice of appeal" -- they were issued within the 12 weeks
        ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF `the date asked about` GREATER THAN `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the 12 weeks have not run out and no grounds are issued yet

@ref O 19 r 30(3)(a) (src:2590)
GIVEN `no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks` IS A BOOLEAN
